With variable-size partitions (slide p. 574), the OS carves memory into exactly as much as each process needs. Free memory becomes a set of holes of various sizes scattered between processes, and each arriving process needs a hole big enough to hold it. Which hole? (Slide p. 575.) First fit : the first hole that's big enough. Best fit : the smallest hole that's big enough; produces the smallest leftover. Worst fit : the largest hole; produces the largest leftover. The slides work an example (pp. 576–587) on a memory of 20 units, with A (4k) at unit 1, B (2k) at 11, and C (3k) at 17 already resident, leaving holes at 5–10, 13–16, and 20. Then D (1k), E (4k), and F (2k) arrive in that order. src/code/sims/fit.ts replays it: interface Hole { start: number; size: number; }
type Strategy = (holes: Hole[], need: number) => Hole | undefined; const firstFit: Strategy = (holes, need) => holes.find((h) => h.size >= need);
const bestFit: Strategy = (holes, need) => holes.filter((h) => h.size >= need).sort((a, b) => a.size - b.size)[0];
const worstFit: Strategy = (holes, need) => holes.filter((h) => h.size >= need).sort((a, b) => b.size - a.size)[0]; $ tsc --target es2022 fit.ts && node fit.js
first fit: D@5 E@6 F@13
best fit: D@20 E@13 F@5
