"An allocation method refers to how disk blocks are assigned to files" (slide p. 769). There are three classic ones, and seeing them side by side makes it obvious how rObSen should grow. Slide p. 771: contiguous allocation. The directory stores start and length.
Slide p. 773: linked allocation. Each block points to the next. Contiguous (pp. 770–771): each file occupies consecutive blocks; the directory records the start and length. Random access is trivial (block i is at start + i ), but it has the same dynamic-storage-allocation problem as memory (first, best, worst fit; external fragmentation; compaction), and files can't grow. Linked (pp. 772–773): each block holds a pointer to the next; the directory records the first (and maybe last). No external fragmentation, files grow freely, but there's no random access (you must follow the chain), pointers cost space, and one corrupted pointer loses the rest of the file. FAT (pp. 774–775) moves all the next-pointers into one table at the start of the disk, which makes following the chain a table lookup instead of a disk read. Slide p. 775: FAT. Block 217 → 618 → 339 → end.
Slide p. 777: indexed allocation. One index block lists all of a file's blocks. Indexed (pp. 776–779): each file has an index block listing all its data blocks; the directory points to the index block. Direct access without external fragmentation, at the cost of an index block per file. Large files need linked index blocks or multiple levels of index; UNIX combines them, with 12 direct pointers in the inode, then single, double, and triple indirect blocks. Slide p. 779: the UNIX inode's combined scheme. rObSen's data blocks already have room for a linked scheme: the TSB in each data block's header is the obvious place for a next pointer. Since T0 S0 B0 can never be a data block, 000 can mean "end of file." A sketch of the write side: public writeFile(name: string, data: string): boolean { const entry = this.findDirectoryEntry(name); if (!entry) return false; const hex = this.stringToHex(data); const perBlock = this.blockSize - this.dataHeaderLength; // 60 hex digits = 30 chars this.freeChainAfter(entry.dataTrack, entry.dataSector, entry.dataBlock); // release the old tail let current = { track: entry.dataTrack, sector: entry.dataSector, block: entry.dataBlock }; for (let offset = 0; offset < Math.max(hex.length, 1); offset += perBlock) { const chunk = hex.slice(offset, offset + perBlock); const isLast = offset + perBlock >= hex.length; // Reserve the next block *before* writing this one, so findFreeDataBlock can't return `current`. const next = isLast ? null : this.reserveDataBlock(); // returns null if the disk is full if (!isLast && !next) return false; const pointer = next ? this.toPointer(next.track, next.sector, next.block) : "000"; this.writeBlock(current.track, current.sector, current.block, `1${pointer}${chunk}`); if (next) current = next; } this.updateDirectoryEntry(entry, { size: data.length }); return true;
} Reading follows the pointers until 000, concatenating chunks. Delete walks the chain to free every block. With 192 data blocks, a file could then be up to 5,760 characters. (reserveDataBlock and freeChainAfter are small helpers left as the exercise; reserveDataBlock is findFreeDataBlock plus immediately writing a 1000… header so the block is marked in use, which is the fix from the bug above, generalized.)

