Now make it concrete. Choose a page size of 32 bytes (2⁵): it divides 256 and 768 evenly and keeps the numbers small. Physical memory: 768 / 32 = 24 frames . Logical address space per process: 256 / 32 = 8 pages , addresses $00–$FF. A logical address splits as page = address >> 5 (top 3 bits) and offset = address & 0x1f (low 5 bits). The PCB loses base, limit, and segment, and gains a page table: public pageTable: number[] = new Array(8).fill(-1); // page → frame, -1 = invalid The memory manager keeps a free-frame list instead of three partitions: class FrameTable { private owner: (number | null)[] = new Array(24).fill(null); // frame → PID allocate(pid: number): number | null { const f = this.owner.indexOf(null); if (f === -1) return null; this.owner[f] = pid; return f; } releaseAll(pid: number): void { this.owner = this.owner.map((o) => (o === pid ? null : o)); }
} The memory accessor's translateAddress changes from base-plus-offset to a table lookup, and gains a real protection check: private translateAddress(address: number, operation: string): number { if (!_CPU?.isExecuting || !_CurrentProcess) return address; // OS: physical if (address < 0 || address > 0xff) { this.handleMemoryViolation(`address 0x${address.toString(16)} outside logical space`); } const page = address >> 5; const offset = address & 0x1f; const frame = _CurrentProcess.pageTable[page]; if (frame === -1) { this.handlePageFault(page, operation); // Chapter 20 } return frame * 32 + offset;
